<h2>2. Detailed Mathematical Formulations & Algorithms</h2>

<h3>2.1. Haversine Great-Circle Geodesic Distance</h3>
<p>Because the Earth is an oblate spheroid, planar Euclidean distance produces catastrophic nautical errors. We compute great-circle distance between departure coordinate (&phi;<sub>1</sub>, &lambda;<sub>1</sub>) and fishing zone (&phi;<sub>2</sub>, &lambda;<sub>2</sub>):</p>
<div class="formula-box">
  &Delta;&phi; = &phi;<sub>2</sub> &minus; &phi;<sub>1</sub>, &nbsp;&nbsp; &Delta;&lambda; = &lambda;<sub>2</sub> &minus; &lambda;<sub>1</sub><br/>
  <strong>a</strong> = sin&sup2;(&Delta;&phi; / 2) + cos(&phi;<sub>1</sub>) &times; cos(&phi;<sub>2</sub>) &times; sin&sup2;(&Delta;&lambda; / 2)<br/>
  <strong>c</strong> = 2 &times; atan2(&radic;a, &radic;(1 &minus; a))<br/>
  <strong>d</strong> = R &times; c &nbsp;&nbsp; <em>(where R = 6,371.0 km Earth Mean Radius)</em>
</div>

<h3>2.2. Initial Forward Navigational Bearing</h3>
<p>Calculates the true compass heading in degrees (0&deg;&ndash;360&deg;) for the vessel's autopilot or helm:</p>
<div class="formula-box">
  x = sin(&Delta;&lambda;) &times; cos(&phi;<sub>2</sub>)<br/>
  y = cos(&phi;<sub>1</sub>) &times; sin(&phi;<sub>2</sub>) &minus; sin(&phi;<sub>1</sub>) &times; cos(&phi;<sub>2</sub>) &times; cos(&Delta;&lambda;)<br/>
  <strong>&theta;</strong> = (degrees(atan2(x, y)) + 360) mod 360
</div>

<h3>2.3. Ray-Casting Algorithm for Maritime Boundary Geofencing</h3>
<p>To enforce International Maritime Boundary Lines (IMBL) and Marine Protected Areas (MPAs) without requiring heavy C-library dependencies on small vessel computers, we implement the <strong>Jordan Curve Point-in-Polygon</strong> algorithm:</p>
<div class="formula-box">
  For a test point (x, y) and polygon vertices (p<sub>i</sub>, p<sub>i+1</sub>):<br/>
  x<sub>inters</sub> = (y &minus; y<sub>1</sub>) &times; (x<sub>2</sub> &minus; x<sub>1</sub>) / (y<sub>2</sub> &minus; y<sub>1</sub>) + x<sub>1</sub><br/>
  <em>If the count of ray intersections with polygon edges is odd, the coordinate strictly lies INSIDE the restricted zone.</em>
</div>

<h3>2.4. A* Risk-Aware Marine Pathfinding Algorithm</h3>
<p>Navigating through open water must penalize physical hazards (shoals, underwater reefs, high swell corridors). Our graph navigator minimizes the cost function:</p>
<div class="formula-box">
  <strong>f(n) = g(n) + h(n) + RiskPenalty(n)</strong><br/>
  &bull; <strong>g(n):</strong> Cumulative nautical distance traversed from departure port.<br/>
  &bull; <strong>h(n):</strong> Haversine heuristic estimate from current cell n to target PFZ coordinate.<br/>
  &bull; <strong>RiskPenalty(n):</strong> Dynamic penalty assigned to sea state cells exhibiting wave heights &gt; 2.0m or coastal hazards.
</div>

<h3>2.5. Deterministic Maritime Risk Score Model (0 to 100)</h3>
<p>The Risk Engine evaluates 5 weighted dimensions with mathematical determinism:</p>
<div class="formula-box">
  <strong>Total Risk (R) = (0.30 &times; R<sub>wave</sub>) + (0.20 &times; R<sub>wind</sub>) + (0.30 &times; R<sub>hazard</sub>) + (0.10 &times; R<sub>distance</sub>) + (0.10 &times; R<sub>geofence</sub>)</strong>
</div>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Wave Risk (30%):</strong> Hs &lt; 0.8m &rarr; 10; 0.8–1.5m &rarr; 25; 1.5–2.2m &rarr; 55; 2.2–3.2m &rarr; 85; &gt; 3.2m &rarr; 100.</li>
  <li><strong>Wind & Squall Risk (20%):</strong> Base wind speed combined with precipitation penalty (Heavy squalls &gt; 5mm/hr add +25 pts).</li>
  <li><strong>Hazard Risk (30%):</strong> Highest active INCOIS/IMD advisory level: LOW &rarr; 30, MODERATE &rarr; 60, HIGH &rarr; 85, SEVERE &rarr; 100.</li>
  <li><strong>Distance & Duration Risk (10%):</strong> Normalized against vessel range: (Dist / 50km &times; 50) + (Duration / 12h &times; 50).</li>
  <li><strong>Geofence Risk (10%):</strong> CLEAR &rarr; 0; NEAR BOUNDARY &rarr; 40; INTERSECTS (Hard Block) &rarr; 100.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout-warning">
  <div class="callout-title">MANDATORY HARD SAFETY FLOOR OVERRIDE</div>
  <p>Regardless of favorable distance or PFZ opportunity, life safety is non-negotiable:</p>
  <p><strong>If Significant Wave Height (Hs) &ge; 2.4m OR Sustained Wind &ge; 42 km/h OR Critical Hazard &ge; 85:</strong><br/>
  <code>Total Risk = max(R, 65.0) &rarr; Automatically forces HIGH RISK Band and advises NO SAIL for small craft.</code></p>
</div>

<h3>2.6. Composite Fishing Suitability Index (0 to 100)</h3>
<p>Combines commercial opportunity with oceanographic viability and navigation safety:</p>
<div class="formula-box">
  <strong>Suitability (S) = 0.35(S<sub>PFZ</sub>) + 0.20(S<sub>Wave</sub>) + 0.20(S<sub>Weather</sub>) + 0.10(S<sub>Hazard</sub>) + 0.10(S<sub>Ocean</sub>) + 0.05(S<sub>Tide</sub>)</strong>
</div>
<ul>
  <li><strong>S<sub>PFZ</sub> (35%):</strong> Satellite confidence score reflecting SST thermal breaks and Chlorophyll-a gradients.</li>
  <li><strong>S<sub>Wave</sub> (20%):</strong> Sea state stability score (calm sea &le; 0.9m scores 92; dangerous sea &gt; 2.4m scores 10).</li>
  <li><strong>S<sub>Weather</sub> (20%):</strong> Wind speed (60%) + Precipitation index (40%).</li>
  <li><strong>S<sub>Ocean</sub> (10%):</strong> Optimal SST window (26.5&deg;C–29.5&deg;C) + Chlorophyll concentration (&ge; 1.0 mg/m&sup3; scores 95).</li>
  <li><strong>S<sub>Tide</sub> (5%):</strong> Survey of India tidal harmonic predictions for shallow bar harbor depth clearance.</li>
</ul>

