<h2>4. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ) for the Jury Viva</h2>

<h3>Q1: "Why did you build an orchestrated multi-agent system instead of a single LLM prompt?"</h3>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>"An all-in-one LLM prompt fails on three counts: (1) <strong>Safety:</strong> LLMs hallucinate numbers and cannot guarantee that wave heights above 2.4m will trigger a hard block; (2) <strong>Latency:</strong> Running heavy GIS coordinates through an LLM prompt increases token costs and latency to 6-8 seconds, whereas our deterministic GIS and Risk engines execute in under 15 milliseconds; (3) <strong>Reproducibility:</strong> Maritime authorities require audit trails where two identical conditions yield identical risk scores."</em></p>

<h3>Q2: "How does ORCA operate when an offshore vessel loses internet connectivity?"</h3>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>"ORCA features a 3-tier offline resilience fallback:</em><br/>
<em>1. If the Gemini API is unreachable, <code>llm_client.py</code> catches the timeout and routes output through our local rule-based Markdown synthesis engine.</em><br/>
<em>2. If external GIS servers are offline, <code>gis_agent.py</code> executes our native, pure-Python ray-casting algorithm.</em><br/>
<em>3. Calibrated Indian coastal gazetteers and cached harmonic tide tables reside directly inside the local engine."</em></p>

<h3>Q3: "How is multi-lingual translation kept faithful without mixing languages?"</h3>
<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>"We enforce a strict single-language contract: <code>ONE USER MESSAGE &rarr; ONE DETECTED LANGUAGE &rarr; ONE COMPLETE RESPONSE IN THAT LANGUAGE</code>. Our NLU agent classifies the script, and the Decision Agent applies strict system prompts forbidding English trailing paragraphs or bilingual mixtures."</em></p>

